my  first memory of  being at whatcom is going in through the  main front doors and  seeing this beautiful beautiful house  like gorgeous building um and thinking  that it might be okay that it might be  safe here and it  might get better here was within the  first few days of me being at whatcom  that i met the abuser  nima's mental health problems began when  she was barely a teenager  she was referred to cams that's the  nhs's mental health service for children  i moved house when i was 14 and  that was when my mental health started  deteriorating so i went from  being sort of quite anxious and  self-harming and having low self-esteem  to um feeling quite suicidal  her treatment eventually led to whatcom  hall  a privately run residential psychiatric  unit  paid for and commissioned by the nhs  but instead of being protected and  helped  nima was exposed to a serial sex  offender  i remember that he used to give me  lots of sort of special treatment so  he'd spend more of his time with me  than with other patients there when he  was on shift and  if i was on one-to-one support so i  needed somebody  with me all the time then he would often  be my one-to-one  so he would find a way to uh  to change or adapt the the shift  and the staff that were on shift so that  he could spend more time  with me did you think anything unnatural  was happening then  not at all not at all i had no idea  that what was happening was grooming  absolutely no idea  right from the beginning with with  marcus i  i felt that i loved him i felt that i  was in love with him in  in the kind of relationship you'd have  between a girlfriend and a boyfriend  not for any minute did i either  think or worry or feel that he was doing  something wrong  nemo was just 16 still a highly  vulnerable  child but despite this her abuser began  to escalate their relationship  the longer i was there and the more  leave that  i was taking with him the more that we  were going on these escorted walks that  the further it progressed and  so it moved on to he  he was kissing me he he kissed me and  and  initially that was sort of like he  kissed me on the cheek and then it  moved to the lips and then it moved  to him touching different parts of my  body nema did not know that the alarm  had been raised about her abuser before  she eventually told a member of staff  what was happening  initially she seemed surprised  but she didn't say that it was  wrong or that it was bad or that it  wasn't  right she seemed surprised how did you  feel after you told her this  i  it's really hard to say exactly  how telling her  made me feel because i'd at the time i  couldn't really recognize  that actually i was telling her because  i needed her help  nemo was discharged in june 2017.  but the most serious assault took place  the day  after she left whatcom hall and arranged  to meet her abuser  in his hometown sheldon it was meant to  be like a  birthday treat for me so he bought me a  necklace  with a little pendant on it and  um he'd brought a little picnic  so we were sat in a woods  near his home and in quite a secluded  area  and we we put this picnic blanket out  and and  we we were citing and chatting and  laughing  and um and then  i took off my top  i just remember feeling sick just  feeling really really sick and having  this  horrible gut feeling that something was  wrong  marcus daniel pleaded guilty to 21  offenses  he was sentenced to 11 years in prison  for sexually abusing  three teenage girls in his care  nema's ordeal did not end after she  raised the alarm about  danielle's abuse her mental health  deteriorated  and she was told to return for treatment  back  to whatcom hall i don't know how  anybody would would have thought that  after  experiencing something so traumatic  that the correct response the right  response the response that would have  been best for me and my mental health  was  to put me back into the environment that  had been so traumatic  but back into the environment where i've  been abused  i was my family where we all just we  felt so  voiceless and confused and hurt and  betrayed  that that there was there felt like no  point in raising objections  it was like the damage had been done  danielle  is responsible for abusing nemo but  she also blames her care provider  the hunter they absolutely failed  in their duty of care that there were so  many  obvious signs and signals and red flags  the entire time that i was  there as a patient at wacom hall there  there were so  many signs that that suggested that that  what was happening between me and marcus  was  was wrong was was horribly wrong  nima is suing the hunticum group at the  time it was owned by four seasons  healthcare group  but isn't anymore in response to nema's  story they told sky news  we deeply regret the harm that miss hunt  experienced  while in the care of whatcom hall which  at the time was owned  and independently managed by the  hunticum group  whatcom hall failed to meet the expected  standards  and was subsequently closed in 2017.  we are unable to comment further on this  specific case  until any legal action has concluded  since the closure of whatcom hall two  other units  run by the huntington group have also  been closed following concerns around  the care provided  another unit was placed into special  measures  at the start of this year the current  owner of the hunticum group has told us  improvements have been made since that  inspection  nema blames an inadequate children's  mental health system  for exposing her to a sexual predator  free to abuse  vulnerable children i don't think i  would have ever needed to go  into psychiatric hospital if  there had been appropriate cam support  in place seeing somebody  once every two weeks was not enough for  me at that time i was really poorly and  i needed help  and i needed somebody to really  be invested in my care nima's case  raises more  serious questions about the care we  provide to our most  vulnerable nema has shown a spotlight  on some profound inadequacies in the the  mental health provision that we give to  children and young people we raised  nima's case  with former health secretary jeremy hunt  he's the chair of the health select  committee  who is holding an inquiry into  children's mental health  clearly there are individuals who did  the most  abhorrent things you have to take full  responsibility under the law  for their care but the system uh  needs improving that is ultimately the  responsibility of the secretary of state  not just matt hancock now but me when i  was secretary of state  and i do believe that huge effort is  being made  to improve the care that people like  nema receive  but sometimes the progress feels  painfully slow  and that is because fundamentally what  we need is a big  expansion of our capacity to deliver  mental health  at all levels nhs england responded to  neima's case by saying  measures are in place so that patients  receive high quality  safe care from the organization's  commission to provide  care and contracts are regularly  monitored  to ensure this happens the nhs  has significantly increased mental  health support for people  in the community an additional 975  million pounds every year will be  invested  in community care by 2023  2024 nima entered into the system as a  vulnerable 13 year old  needing help with her mental health  she's now 20.  i have a diagnosis now of complex  post-traumatic  stress disorder and as a result of of  what i lived through  at whatcom hall of the abuse i endured  there  i still self-harm  the severity of my self-harm has has  really changed since  i've been at whatcom and  i struggle more than i ever have now  with  with my body in its physical form  and the idea of having a woman's body  feels really uh  almost sickening to me because of  because of what marcus did  because of what he made me believe about  myself

